  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The modulus changes formula at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, the integrand is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, so the integral is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Setting this equal to 3 gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, the area accumulated up to 3 is 3, and the remaining integrand is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. Thus the total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Equating this to 3 gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>. The shared boundary is one value, not two.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The distinct positive values are 1,3,5, whose sum is 9. Answer D. Negative portions of the integrand count negatively; this is a signed integral rather than total positive area.</p>"
}
